Dear statnet users,


I have collected  friendship network data for *multiple classes* in
different schools.

*I would like to:*

*1)* compare the indegree of some students that took a course with those
who didn?t took it, controlling for some variables and baseline indegree
using MRQAP.

?*Course*? is an attribute of each node and is equal to ?1? for those who
took the course, ?0? for those who didn?t took it and ?NA? for those who
didn?t know about it.


*However, the problem is that:*
*A)* since students were asked to nominate only classmates I have multiple
networks not a single big network.
*B)* in some of them I have just one student who is either part of the
?course group? or the ?non-course group?. Therefore, I don't think running
MRQAP for each class and then using meta-analysis will work.


The other option that I found is to use "structural zeros" to create a
single big matrix and run MRQAP, but since I?m very new in R and I couldn?t
find examples I don?t have any idea on how it should be done.


*2)* I would like to know for each node how many nominations out of the
total nominations  (freeman) are from and to students who took the course.
Stated in a different way, I want to answer to the following question: ?*How
many of your friends took the course??*

I have tried to use the degree function and the given options but with no
success


Below you can find an example with 3 classes in one single network and
node?s attributes including:

*class*=The class id, from 1 to 3

*course*=course status coded

*1* for students that took the course

*0* for those who did not take the course

*NA* for those who did not know about it


#########################

library(statnet)

set.seed(123)

mynet<-network.initialize(60, directed=TRUE)

mynet[1:20,1:20] <- rbinom(100, 1, 0.15)

mynet[21:34,21:34] <- rbinom(120, 1, 0.15)

mynet[35:60,35:60] <- rbinom(96, 1, 0.15)

#class id

class<-60

class[1:20]<-1

class[21:34]<-2

class[35:60]<-3

# course variable:

course<-rbinom(60,c(1,0,NA),0.5)

mynet %v% "class"<-class

mynet %v% " course "<- course

list.vertex.attributes(mynet)


