The three matrices represent annual pair-wise distances between pairs of
nodes. I'm estimating how these distances impact tie-formation (and how
they change over time).

I am not sure if these measures can be generated from nodal information
because they rely on data that is not available in the network. For
instance, one of the measure is the euclidean distance between pairs of
nodes measured in the network of previous years.

Does anyone know how to deal with this?

Any help is much appreciated. Thank you!

Frank van der Wouden


On Mon, Feb 26, 2018 at 11:35 PM, Pavel Krivitsky <pavel@uow.edu.au> wrote:

> Dear Frank,
>
> *TL;DR:* The edgecov() terms are very memory-inefficient. I don't know
> what these mg/m4/mt matrices represent, but if they can be generated from,
> say, nodal information, you can implement a custom ergm term that gets
> passed nodal information and generates them on the fly.
>
> *Technical explanation: *Internally, ergm concatenates covariate vectors
> and passes them on to the C code, where they get unpacked. Each edgecov()
> term passes an n*n matrix, and in your case, each of these matrices is
> 270000^2 elements long. Three of them add up to a vector of length
> 2187000000 <(218)%20700-0000> which is longer than the greatest integer
> that can be represented using the basic integer type (2147483647
> <(214)%20748-3647>). Now, R can handle vectors like that, but not through
> the .C() interface.
>
> I hope this helps,
> Pavel
>
> On Mon, 2018-02-26 at 17:19 +0100, FRANK VAN DER WOUDEN wrote:
>
